WEBVTT

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:07.078
Here's a table of two million orders, and one query: count a customer's orders, and add up the amounts.

00:00:07.328 --> 00:00:12.197
For customer 4242, it takes fifteen microseconds to execute.

00:00:12.447 --> 00:00:16.577
For customer one, it takes a hundred and thirty-five milliseconds.

00:00:16.627 --> 00:00:18.663
About nine thousand times longer.

00:00:19.513 --> 00:00:21.996
Same query, same table, same machine.

00:00:22.046 --> 00:00:24.514
And nothing in the query says how to run it.

00:00:24.764 --> 00:00:26.018
So who decides?

00:00:26.068 --> 00:00:29.830
And why is the same query sometimes fast, and sometimes slow?

00:00:33.580 --> 00:00:40.337
In ordinary code, you'd write the search yourself: loop over every order, and keep the ones for this customer.

00:00:40.386 --> 00:00:42.213
The code says exactly how.

00:00:42.463 --> 00:00:44.120
SQL is declarative.

00:00:44.170 --> 00:00:47.941
The query says what rows you want, and nothing about how to find them.

00:00:48.191 --> 00:00:50.546
That leaves the database free to choose how.

00:00:50.596 --> 00:00:53.651
And it has to choose, every time the query runs.

00:00:55.201 --> 00:00:59.967
The table is stored in pages, blocks of about a hundred and sixty rows each.

00:01:00.017 --> 00:01:03.819
Two million orders fill about twelve thousand seven hundred of them.

00:01:04.069 --> 00:01:07.674
There are two basic ways to find one customer's orders.

00:01:07.924 --> 00:01:12.352
One is a full scan: read every page, and keep the rows that match.

00:01:12.402 --> 00:01:15.470
It reads the same pages whoever the customer is.

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:22.601
The other is an index: a sorted list of customer numbers, each pointing to where that customer's rows are.

00:01:22.651 --> 00:01:28.266
Find the customer in the list, collect where their rows are, and read only the pages that hold them.

00:01:28.516 --> 00:01:32.527
Customer 4242 has ten orders, on ten pages.

00:01:32.577 --> 00:01:37.036
Through the index, that's ten pages instead of twelve thousand seven hundred.

00:01:38.086 --> 00:01:41.423
Customer one has about six hundred thousand orders.

00:01:41.473 --> 00:01:49.741
Orders are stored in the order they arrived, not by customer, so on every page, about three rows in ten are customer one's.

00:01:49.791 --> 00:01:51.946
Now the index can't skip anything.

00:01:51.996 --> 00:01:54.242
Every page has to be read anyway.

00:01:55.292 --> 00:01:59.131
So whether the index saves work depends on how many rows match.

00:01:59.181 --> 00:02:01.027
And that depends on the customer.

00:02:01.977 --> 00:02:04.955
The choice between them is made by the query planner.

00:02:05.205 --> 00:02:13.426
The planner keeps statistics about each table: how many rows it has, and for each column, which values are most common, and how common.

00:02:13.676 --> 00:02:24.991
For each way it could run the query, it estimates the rows and pages it would touch, turns that into a cost, a number for comparing plans rather than a time, and picks the cheapest.

00:02:25.241 --> 00:02:28.924
For customer 4242, the statistics say rare.

00:02:28.974 --> 00:02:34.545
The planner estimates thirty-three rows; the real number is ten, close enough to choose well.

00:02:34.595 --> 00:02:41.396
Its cost for using the index comes to about a hundred and thirty; for a full scan, about thirty-eight thousand.

00:02:41.446 --> 00:02:50.101
So it picks the index, read the way we just saw: collect the rows' locations, sort them by page, then read each of those pages once.

00:02:50.151 --> 00:02:57.396
PostgreSQL calls that a bitmap scan, shown as two steps: one reads the index, the other reads the pages.

00:02:57.446 --> 00:02:59.819
Ten pages, fifteen microseconds.

00:03:00.869 --> 00:03:07.978
Force a full scan for the same customer, and it takes seventy-two milliseconds: nearly five thousand times slower.

00:03:08.028 --> 00:03:09.305
The choice matters.

00:03:10.355 --> 00:03:14.346
For customer one, the statistics say about three in ten orders.

00:03:14.396 --> 00:03:20.043
The planner estimates about six hundred thousand rows, and picks the same kind of bitmap scan.

00:03:20.093 --> 00:03:22.952
But this time the pages it reads are all of them.

00:03:23.002 --> 00:03:25.482
A hundred and thirty-five milliseconds.

00:03:25.532 --> 00:03:33.585
Force a full scan instead, and it's no faster: it reads the same pages, and still has six hundred thousand rows to add up.

00:03:34.635 --> 00:03:40.023
When the rows you want are on every page, the index doesn't help, and no plan is fast.

00:03:41.773 --> 00:03:43.933
Everything so far read one table.

00:03:43.983 --> 00:03:47.224
Now ask for the orders of every customer in Tromsø.

00:03:47.274 --> 00:03:51.731
Two tables have to meet, and the planner has to choose how to match their rows.

00:03:51.981 --> 00:03:55.936
A hundred customers live in Tromsø, out of a hundred thousand.

00:03:56.186 --> 00:04:02.167
The planner finds those hundred customers, then, for each one, looks up its orders in the index.

00:04:02.217 --> 00:04:07.047
That's a nested loop: for each row on one side, look up its matches on the other.

00:04:07.097 --> 00:04:08.765
About five milliseconds.

00:04:09.615 --> 00:04:13.982
Now ask for customers in Oslo: almost all of the hundred thousand.

00:04:14.032 --> 00:04:21.326
This time the planner builds a lookup table from the Oslo customers, and streams all two million orders through it, once.

00:04:21.376 --> 00:04:22.711
That's a hash join.

00:04:22.761 --> 00:04:26.651
About seven hundred milliseconds, for almost two million rows.

00:04:26.901 --> 00:04:32.868
Force a nested loop for Oslo instead, and it takes two seconds: nearly three times as long.

00:04:33.918 --> 00:04:35.240
Same query shape.

00:04:35.290 --> 00:04:38.646
A different plan, because a different number of rows match.

00:04:40.196 --> 00:04:44.843
The plan is only as good as the estimate, and the statistics are a snapshot.

00:04:45.093 --> 00:04:47.088
Here's a copy of the orders table.

00:04:47.138 --> 00:04:51.847
Its statistics were gathered while customer one still had three in ten orders.

00:04:51.897 --> 00:04:58.512
Since then, their older orders were archived, and just two hundred and ninety-four are left, all of them recent.

00:04:58.762 --> 00:05:02.512
Now ask for customer one's first ten orders, by order number.

00:05:02.762 --> 00:05:08.618
The planner still believes customer one is everywhere: about four hundred and twenty thousand rows.

00:05:08.668 --> 00:05:14.597
So it walks through the orders by order number, expecting to run into ten of theirs almost at once.

00:05:14.647 --> 00:05:16.679
But all of theirs are at the very end.

00:05:16.729 --> 00:05:20.012
It passes about one point four million other rows first.

00:05:20.062 --> 00:05:22.645
A hundred and thirty-seven milliseconds.

00:05:23.695 --> 00:05:30.707
Run ANALYZE, which refreshes the statistics, and the planner now expects about three hundred and thirty rows.

00:05:30.757 --> 00:05:34.576
It looks them up in the index, sorts them, and keeps the first ten.

00:05:34.626 --> 00:05:39.556
Sixty microseconds: more than two thousand times faster, for the same query.

00:05:40.606 --> 00:05:44.074
The usual fix for a slow query is to add an index.

00:05:44.124 --> 00:05:48.737
But nobody added one here: the one on customer number was there all along.

00:05:48.787 --> 00:05:51.079
The planner just didn't expect it to help.

00:05:51.929 --> 00:05:53.719
That's how to read a slow plan.

00:05:53.769 --> 00:05:59.993
EXPLAIN ANALYZE shows, for each step, the rows the planner expected and the rows it actually got.

00:06:00.043 --> 00:06:04.492
Where the two are far apart, the plan was chosen for data that isn't there.

00:06:06.242 --> 00:06:08.250
So who decides how a query runs?

00:06:08.300 --> 00:06:11.678
The query planner, because SQL only says what you want.

00:06:11.928 --> 00:06:19.346
It weighs the ways it could run the query by how many rows and pages each step will touch, using statistics about your data.

00:06:19.396 --> 00:06:22.503
A rare customer gets the index, and ten pages.

00:06:22.553 --> 00:06:26.508
For a common one, every page has to be read, whatever the plan.

00:06:26.558 --> 00:06:32.042
A few matches, with an index to look them up, get a nested loop; many get a hash join.

00:06:32.292 --> 00:06:40.071
That's why the same query can be fast or slow: it depends on the data it touches, and on what the planner believes about that data.

00:06:40.321 --> 00:06:44.000
When a query is slow, don't guess, and don't just add an index.

00:06:44.050 --> 00:06:49.356
Run EXPLAIN ANALYZE, and compare the rows the planner expected with the rows it got.
